1) Optimized Linux File System Layout for a 50 GB Server
 ANS:


var         → 8 GB
log     → 10 GB separate filesystem
home        → 7 GB
opt         → 8 GB
The server has 50 GB storage and must handle system logs, user files and application data. The main problem is that rapidly growing logs may consume all disk space and stop critical services.

A suitable allocation is:

Partition	Size	Purpose
boot	1 GB	Kernel and boot files
8 GB	Operating system
home	7 GB	User files
var	8 GB	Variable application/system data
var/log	10 GB	System and application logs
opt	8 GB	Application software/data
Swap	4 GB	Memory support
Reserved	4 GB	Emergency expansion
Total	50 GB	

The most important decision is to create a separate 10 GB /var/log filesystem. Therefore, even if logs grow rapidly, they cannot consume the space required by / or /home.
Use logrotate to control log growth:
sudo logrotate /etc/logrotate.conf
A logrotate policy can rotate logs daily, compress old logs and retain only a fixed number of files.
Disk usage can be monitored using:
df -h
du -sh /var/log
Systemd journal usage can also be limited:
journalctl --vacuum-size=2G
LVM is recommended because logical volumes can be expanded more easily when additional storage is added.

Conclusion:

*Separate /var/log prevents logs from filling the root filesystem.
*logrotate controls and compresses old log files.
*Monitoring detects high disk usage early.
*LVM provides flexible storage management.
*Critical services remain protected from disk-space exhaustion.
