Q2. Reducing Context-Switching Delays in Linux:
ANS)
Frequent context switching occurs when the CPU repeatedly changes between processes or threads. This introduces scheduling overhead and can reduce performance, especially for real-time applications.

First measure the problem:
vmstat 1
pidstat -w 1
top
vmstat displays context switches, while pidstat identifies processes producing excessive switching.
CPU affinity can be used to keep an important application on selected CPUs:
taskset -c 2,3 ./realtime_app
This can improve cache locality and reduce CPU migration.
Process priority can be changed using:
sudo renice -n -10 -p PID
For reaL-time applications, Linux real-time scheduling can be used:
sudo chrt -f 80 
SCHED_FIFO gives the application real-time scheduling priority, but it must be used carefully because a badly configured real-time process can starve other processes.
Unnecessary background services should also be reduced:
systectl list-units --type=service
For demanding real-time workloads, a PREEMPT_RT kernel and CPU isolation can reduce scheduling latency.
Kernel modules can be inspected using:

lsmod

and loaded when required using:

sudo modprobe module_name

Conclusion: Measure first, then use CPU affinity, priority tuning, real-time scheduling, CPU isolation and an appropriate real-time kernel. These techniques reduce context-switching overhead without requiring hardware upgrades.
