Q3. Processing 1 Million Log Entries Within 2 Minutes
Processing one million records using shell scripting requires minimizing process creation and avoiding repeated scans of the same file.

A poor approach is:

while read line
do
    echo "$line" | grep ERROR
done < logfile

This is slow because external commands may be created repeatedly.

Instead, use awk, which can process the entire file in a single pass:

awk '/ERROR/ {count++} END {print "Errors:", count}' logfile

To extract error entries:

awk '/ERROR/ {print}' logfile > errors.log

For simple fixed-string searches, use:

grep -Fc 'ERROR' logfile

Avoid unnecessary commands such as:

cat logfile | grep ERROR

Use:

grep ERROR logfile

If several types of entries must be counted, process the file only once:

awk '
  {error++}
 {warning++}
  {info++}
END {
 print "ERROR:",error
 print "WARNING:",warning
 print "INFO:",info
}' logfile

This is faster because the million-line file is scanned once rather than several times.

Execution time should be tested using:

time ./process_logs.sh

The real execution time must be below 120 seconds.

Conclusion: awk is the best choice for complex filtering, counting and transformation because it processes records efficiently in a single pass. grep is suitable for simple searches. Avoid per-line external commands and repeated file scans to meet the 2-minute requirement.
